WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.779
Every reaction in this module comes down to one question. Where do the electrons go?

00:00:06.378 --> 00:00:17.622
In a redox reaction, one substance loses electrons and another substance gains them. The electrons lost are exactly the electrons gained. One cannot happen without the other.

00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:25.226
Oxidation is the loss of electrons. Reduction is the gain of electrons.

00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:34.821
The substance that loses electrons is the reducing agent. It donates electrons, so it reduces something else, and it is itself oxidised.

00:00:35.421 --> 00:00:44.368
The substance that gains electrons is the oxidising agent. It accepts electrons, so it oxidises something else, and it is itself reduced.

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:52.794
In this studio, blue always means oxidation and the reducing agent. Red always means reduction and the oxidising agent.

00:00:55.294 --> 00:01:02.271
Here is zinc reacting with copper ions. Which species is the oxidising agent? Decide now.

00:01:06.871 --> 00:01:14.841
It is the copper two plus ion. It accepts two electrons from the zinc, so it is reduced, and it is the oxidising agent.

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:25.589
Not zinc: zinc is oxidised, so zinc is the reducing agent. And not copper metal: copper is a product. Always name a reactant as the agent.

00:01:28.089 --> 00:01:32.440
Now open Table 4. Every row is a reduction half-reaction.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:43.808
On the left of each row is an oxidising agent. As you go down the table, the oxidising agents get stronger. Fluorine, at the bottom, is the strongest.

00:01:44.408 --> 00:01:54.630
On the right of each row is a reducing agent. As you go up the table, the reducing agents get stronger. Lithium, at the top, is the strongest.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:04.670
To write a reduction, copy the whole row with a single arrow. Copper two plus, plus two electrons, gives copper.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:14.686
To write an oxidation, write the whole row backwards. Zinc gives zinc two plus, plus two electrons. The electrons are now on the right.

00:02:15.286 --> 00:02:18.708
Then check that the atoms and the charges balance on both sides.

00:02:21.208 --> 00:02:33.233
When you balance electrons, you may need to multiply a half-reaction by two or three. You never multiply the electrode potential. The value belongs to the half-cell, not to the number of electrons.

00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:47.230
Now open the half-reaction builder and write three half-reactions of your own. Copy the row for a reduction. Write it backwards for an oxidation. And keep following the electrons.
